    rdfs:label "Evidence for Sulphuric Acid used in Hat-Making, Brushes, Upholstery & Turnery: Mortimer (1823), General Commercial Dictionary, scan p. 490"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "This is done at an apparatus called a battery, consisting of a kettle (containing water slightly acidulated with sulphuric acid, to which, for beaver hats, a quantity of wine-lees or the grounds of beer is added, or else plain water for rinsing out), and eight planks of wood joined together in the form of a frustum of a cone, and meeting in the kettle at the..."@en ;
